1
00:00:00,650 --> 00:00:05,870
11 March 2011. 2:46 in the afternoon.

2
00:00:06,220 --> 00:00:09,120
First the ground shook. Then the sea rose.

3
00:00:09,470 --> 00:00:12,590
And then a nuclear plant lost its power.

4
00:00:12,940 --> 00:00:15,220
This is the story of Fukushima.

5
00:00:21,820 --> 00:00:23,225
The east coast of Japan.

6
00:00:23,245 --> 00:00:27,660
On the shore of the Pacific, the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant.

7
00:00:28,010 --> 00:00:33,110
Six reactors. A large share of Tokyo's electricity came from here.

8
00:00:33,460 --> 00:00:40,620
That day, units 1, 2 and 3 were running. Units 4, 5 and 6 were shut for maintenance.

9
00:00:40,970 --> 00:00:46,550
All of unit 4's fuel had been taken out of the reactor and placed in a pool of water.

10
00:00:46,900 --> 00:00:50,560
At the entrance to the nearby town of Futaba stood a sign.

11
00:00:50,910 --> 00:00:53,890
"Nuclear power: energy for a bright future."

12
00:00:59,290 --> 00:01:04,950
2:46 in the afternoon. Under the sea, two plates of the earth slipped.

13
00:01:05,300 --> 00:01:10,160
Magnitude 9. The biggest earthquake in Japan's recorded history.

14
00:01:10,510 --> 00:01:13,290
The ground shook for about six minutes.

15
00:01:13,640 --> 00:01:18,640
The plant's reactors shut down automatically. The control rods went in.

16
00:01:18,990 --> 00:01:23,950
Power from outside was cut. Then the emergency diesel generators started.

17
00:01:24,300 --> 00:01:27,825
In the control room, everything was still under control.

18
00:01:27,845 --> 00:01:30,260
But the real danger was in the sea.

19
00:01:35,660 --> 00:01:39,760
About fifty minutes after the earthquake, the sea began to draw back.

20
00:01:40,110 --> 00:01:43,650
Then, one after another, the tsunami waves came.

21
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,800
The plant had been designed for a wave of about six metres.

22
00:01:48,150 --> 00:01:52,070
But the wave that came was fourteen to fifteen metres high.

23
00:01:52,420 --> 00:01:55,436
Water filled the basements of the turbine buildings.

24
00:01:55,456 --> 00:01:57,700
That is where the generators stood.

25
00:01:58,050 --> 00:02:00,990
One by one, all the generators stopped.

26
00:02:06,390 --> 00:02:09,530
Units 1 to 4 lost all power.

27
00:02:09,880 --> 00:02:13,560
The control room went dark. The instruments died.

28
00:02:13,910 --> 00:02:19,010
The reactors were shut down, but the fuel was still giving off tremendous heat.

29
00:02:19,360 --> 00:02:24,460
Keeping it cool needed pumps. And pumps needed electricity.

30
00:02:24,810 --> 00:02:29,910
Workers took batteries out of cars in the car park, so they could read the gauges.

31
00:02:30,260 --> 00:02:34,100
Inside the reactors, the water was boiling away.

32
00:02:39,500 --> 00:02:43,920
As the water fell, the fuel rods were exposed to the air.

33
00:02:44,270 --> 00:02:47,130
The temperature went above two thousand degrees.

34
00:02:47,480 --> 00:02:52,000
The fuel began to melt. First in unit 1, then in units 2 and 3.

35
00:02:52,350 --> 00:02:56,790
The reaction between hot metal and steam began to make hydrogen gas.

36
00:02:57,140 --> 00:03:01,140
The gas kept collecting under the roof of the reactor building.

37
00:03:06,540 --> 00:03:09,800
12 March, 3:36 in the afternoon.

38
00:03:10,150 --> 00:03:13,490
A hydrogen explosion tore through the unit 1 building.

39
00:03:13,840 --> 00:03:17,600
The roof was blown off. Radioactive dust spread into the air.

40
00:03:17,950 --> 00:03:22,850
The evacuation zone kept growing. Two kilometres, then ten, then twenty.

41
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:28,160
People left with a single bag. They thought it would be a matter of a few days.

42
00:03:33,560 --> 00:03:37,700
14 March. An even bigger explosion in unit 3.

43
00:03:38,050 --> 00:03:41,530
15 March. The unit 4 building blew apart too.

44
00:03:41,880 --> 00:03:44,259
Even though its reactor was empty.

45
00:03:44,279 --> 00:03:48,500
The hydrogen had come from the next unit, through a pipe.

46
00:03:48,850 --> 00:03:53,030
The same day, unit 2 released the most radiation of all.

47
00:03:53,380 --> 00:03:58,880
The wind was blowing to the north-west. The radiation drifted towards the villages.

48
00:04:04,280 --> 00:04:07,400
Most of the workers were pulled out.

49
00:04:07,750 --> 00:04:12,990
But some stayed behind. The world called them the Fukushima 50.

50
00:04:13,340 --> 00:04:18,640
They took turns going into the dark buildings, to open valves by hand.

51
00:04:18,990 --> 00:04:25,910
Plant manager Masao Yoshida kept pumping sea water into the reactor, despite orders from above.

52
00:04:26,260 --> 00:04:32,740
He knew the reactor would be ruined for good. But there was no other way.

53
00:04:38,140 --> 00:04:43,340
17 March. Army helicopters began dropping sea water from above.

54
00:04:43,690 --> 00:04:47,930
The radiation was so strong they could not stay over the plant for long.

55
00:04:48,280 --> 00:04:52,540
Then came fire engines, and concrete pump trucks with long booms.

56
00:04:52,890 --> 00:04:55,436
The biggest fear was the unit 4 pool.

57
00:04:55,456 --> 00:04:59,470
If it dried out, the fuel could catch fire in the open.

58
00:04:59,820 --> 00:05:03,480
Even a plan to evacuate Tokyo had been discussed.

59
00:05:08,880 --> 00:05:12,560
About one hundred and fifty thousand people had to leave their homes.

60
00:05:12,910 --> 00:05:16,990
Schools, shops, farms. All left just as they were.

61
00:05:17,340 --> 00:05:20,860
Cows and pets wandered the streets.

62
00:05:21,210 --> 00:05:26,070
Poisoned soil was scraped up and packed into black bags. Millions of bags.

63
00:05:26,420 --> 00:05:31,360
In April, Japan rated it level 7. The same as Chernobyl.

64
00:05:36,760 --> 00:05:41,500
To keep the melted fuel cool, water is still poured in today.

65
00:05:41,850 --> 00:05:47,570
That water becomes radioactive. It was stored in more than a thousand tanks.

66
00:05:47,920 --> 00:05:53,440
To hold back the groundwater, the soil was frozen into a wall of ice.

67
00:05:53,790 --> 00:05:58,510
In 2023, treated water began to be released into the sea.

68
00:05:58,860 --> 00:06:01,434
Robots were sent inside the reactors.

69
00:06:01,454 --> 00:06:05,300
Many broke down on the way, ruined by the radiation.

70
00:06:10,700 --> 00:06:15,610
Just a hundred kilometres away, the Onagawa plant stood on higher ground.

71
00:06:15,630 --> 00:06:16,640
It survived.

72
00:06:16,990 --> 00:06:23,450
There had been warnings before. A big tsunami had been predicted years earlier.

73
00:06:23,800 --> 00:06:29,060
After Fukushima, Germany decided to shut down its nuclear plants.

74
00:06:29,410 --> 00:06:33,490
Cleaning up the plant completely will take thirty to forty years.

75
00:06:33,840 --> 00:06:38,060
Chernobyl showed what human error can do.

76
00:06:38,410 --> 00:06:43,630
Fukushima taught that, in front of nature, any wall can turn out to be too small.

